WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.425
A work breakdown structure organizes scope into a
hierarchy of deliverables and work packages.

00:00:06.545 --> 00:00:10.570
It answers what is included before you decide all the
dates.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:16.915
Our portal example has discovery, design, implementation,
testing and release branches.

00:00:17.035 --> 00:00:25.585
In this lesson you will add and edit nodes, move a whole
subtree, choose branch presentation, create a
relationship, and style a selection.

00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:32.680
Keep the distinction between scope structure and schedule
logic in mind; the connected Gantt workflow comes next.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.750
Each extra hierarchy marker places a node one level
deeper.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:47.420
The source makes the parent-child structure visible as
text, while the diagram makes it easier to discuss.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.265
Select Discovery and identify its interviews and
requirements children.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:56.260
A parent label should describe a coherent part of the
work.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:07.705
Avoid turning the hierarchy into a list of unrelated
status notes, and remember that rearranging the scope does
not by itself assign dates or prove dependencies between
all its leaves.

00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.458
Use Insert and WBS node to add another package.

00:01:15.578 --> 00:01:21.828
Give it an outcome-oriented label, such as Support
training, and review where it belongs in the structure.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:28.348
A new node should have a meaningful parent rather than
appearing wherever there happened to be space on the
canvas.

00:01:28.468 --> 00:01:32.168
Inspect the resulting source markers and the drawing.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:39.913
Later, when we link this WBS to a Gantt chart, those scope
decisions will help us identify missing scheduled work.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:48.033
Select Discovery and change its label to Discover customer
needs.

00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:51.003
Leave the field to apply the edit.

00:01:51.123 --> 00:01:56.723
The node keeps its children, so a clearer label does not
require rebuilding the branch.

00:01:56.843 --> 00:02:00.843
Branch side changes where work appears around the parent.

00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:04.863
Move this branch to the left and compare the source and
preview.

00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:13.358
Use side and color choices to improve readability, but
keep the hierarchy itself responsible for explaining which
work belongs together.

00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:22.617
Dragging a node onto the middle of another node nests the
complete subtree under that target.

00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:28.712
Dropping above or below reorders, and dropping to the
sides changes branch presentation.

00:02:28.832 --> 00:02:35.007
Move Discovery into Design in this disposable example and
inspect the children afterward.

00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:38.802
Interviews and requirements should travel with their
parent.

00:02:38.922 --> 00:02:43.347
Use Undo when the new grouping does not express the
intended scope.

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:49.442
A good hierarchy is one you can explain, not simply a
symmetrical arrangement of boxes.

00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:56.942
The WBS also supports keyboard interaction.

00:02:57.062 --> 00:03:02.412
Focus a node and use the supported alternate-arrow
movement to reorder siblings.

00:03:02.532 --> 00:03:07.432
Here we move Design upward among its siblings, then
inspect the source order.

00:03:07.552 --> 00:03:14.702
Keyboard focus matters: a shortcut inside a text field
edits that field rather than moving a diagram node.

00:03:14.822 --> 00:03:23.197
Use the diagram's focusable controls deliberately, and
return to Help when you need the exact shortcut for your
platform and current editor.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.650
A WBS relationship expresses an additional connection
beyond the tree hierarchy.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:39.720
Focus Discovery and start keyboard connection mode with C,
then focus Implementation and press Enter.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.240
Inspect the new arrow and generated source.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.135
You can also use the selected node's connection handle.

00:03:47.255 --> 00:03:53.380
Keep this meaning separate from parenthood: adding an
arrow does not move a node into another branch.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:04:00.025
When links later map to scheduling dependencies, review
any mappings that cannot be represented safely.

00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:08.783
Select Discovery and Design with a modifier so both belong
to the selection.

00:04:08.903 --> 00:04:13.403
The bulk panel lists operations supported by the selected
objects.

00:04:13.523 --> 00:04:17.348
Apply one shared style and inspect the source changes.

00:04:17.468 --> 00:04:26.118
Bulk operations are useful for consistent presentation,
but review which objects are included before copying or
duplicating a selection.

00:04:26.238 --> 00:04:34.638
Use labels and structure to communicate meaning as well as
color, and avoid treating a styling change as progress or
schedule information.

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:42.642
Pause and add a Support training work package to the
appropriate branch.

00:04:42.762 --> 00:04:48.862
Rename one parent for clarity, move a subtree, and confirm
that its children remain attached.

00:04:48.982 --> 00:04:53.857
Create one additional relationship and explain how it
differs from the tree.

00:04:53.977 --> 00:04:58.952
Finally, apply a shared style to two nodes and inspect the
changed source.

00:04:59.072 --> 00:05:06.822
Your success check is semantic: the work is organized
under sensible parents and the additional connection has a
clear purpose.

00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:15.983
You can now maintain the WBS as a structured model rather
than a static picture.

00:05:16.103 --> 00:05:22.753
Read the hierarchy, edit nodes, move subtrees, use
keyboard controls, and review extra relationships.

00:05:22.873 --> 00:05:30.123
The next lesson places diagrams inside one portable
document and connects WBS work to a Gantt schedule.

00:05:30.243 --> 00:05:36.043
We will then check coverage so that a work package is not
silently missing from the delivery plan.
